   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hi Phil, </div><div><br></div><div>I&#39;m hoping you can clear up my understanding of the simulation example provided for the mirt::simdata function for a graded model. The code is as follows: </div><div><br></div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">a </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rlnorm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">20</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br><br>diffs </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> t</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">apply</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">runif</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">20</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">20</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> cumsum</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>diffs </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">diffs </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> rowMeans</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">diffs</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>d </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> diffs </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> rnorm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">20</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br><br>dat </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> simdata</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">a</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> d</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">500</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> itemtype </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;graded&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div></code></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>What I&#39;m not understanding about the example is that when I run a mirt model and look at the item trace plots it looks like the thresholds are sampled heavily at the tails (see example). In the process of applied work, you might conclude that the middle categories of the rating scale do not distinguish adequately for these items. I&#39;m stuck on what the next interim step would be, using this approach, to enforce more separation between the categories and more peaked probability curves?</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks for any insights</div><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"><img src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/mirt-package/attach/a756f76e4eb3b/OCCs.png?part=0.1&amp;view=1\" alt=\"OCCs.png\" width=\"320\" height=\"168\" style=\"margin-left:1em;margin-right:1em\"></p><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "kVj_cTUTAAAJ",
   "from": "Phil Chalmers",
   "author": "b330c2028fed415b",
   "date": "2019-11-21T02:08:09Z",
   "subject": "Re: Enforcing more separation of the thresholds in the GRM example for the simdata function",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">This was just a minimal example, you can change the width of the intervals just by changing the intercept differences. So <div><br></div><div>diffs &lt;- t(apply(matrix(runif(20*4, 1, 1), 20), 1, cumsum))</div><div><br></div><div>would be much wider and allow for more likelihood in each category based on the expected probability functions. If it&#39;s helpful try playing around with the itemplot(shiny=TRUE) interface with the &#39;graded&#39; model. HTH.<br><div><br clear=\"all\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_signature\" data-smartmail=\"gmail_signature\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Phil</div></div></div></div><br></div></div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Wed, Nov 20, 2019 at 1:50 PM B &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">brandon....@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
